# SHARED HOST ONLY — leave this commented out on a development machine. It opts the profiled `caddy`
# service in, and Caddy is meaningful only where the public hostname actually resolves; elsewhere it
# retries ACME for api.lrnki.globesoul.com forever against the real VPS. The deploy script names
# `caddy` explicitly, so it deploys correctly with or without this set.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=public

# Host port Postgres publishes on. Single definition: docker-compose interpolates it for the
# publish, and the DSNs below carry it for host-run processes. Container-internal stays 5432.
POSTGRES_HOST_PORT=5433
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://lrnki:lrnki@localhost:5433/lrnki
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8881,http://127.0.0.1:8881

# Learner identity and sessions (ADR-0041). No compose default by design — the API refuses to boot
# on an empty `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET`, and the ADR's consequences say why that is the safe failure.
# Mint one with `openssl rand -base64 32`; rotating it signs every learner out (README → Deployment).
# `BETTER_AUTH_URL` is the API's own origin and must match the redirect URI registered on the
# web-type Google OAuth client (`${BETTER_AUTH_URL}/auth/callback/google`); one such client serves
# web and native. Leave the Google pair blank to run the email/password path alone — every rig does.
# The value below is the LOCAL DEV origin. On a deployment host it must be that host's public API
# origin instead: copying this line as-is deploys an API that passes every health check while Google
# rejects the callback and the session cookie loses `Secure` (ADR-0041). Deleting the line entirely
# is safe — compose then supplies the deployed default — and `scripts/deploy-learner-api.sh` fails
# the deploy if what shipped disagrees with the origin it serves.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:8787
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
